-- A type is usually WRITTEN as a definition, and its former shows
-- only after head EXPOSURE — `bisim s t` in the corpus is a squash,
-- and nothing about the folded spelling says so. Classification runs
-- on the EXPOSED context; what is printed back is the folded one,
-- which is what the operator reads.
def sq : ℕ → Ω ≔ λn. ∥ℕ∥

def alias : 𝕌 ≔ ℕ ⊎ ℕ

-- the elaborator follows the same unfolding only where the item CITES
-- it, so this one goes through
def cited : (n : ℕ) → sq n → sq n using (sq.unfold) ≔ λn. λh. ?g1

-- and this one does not: the trial refuses it, and names the citation
-- that would let it through
def bare : (n : ℕ) → sq n → sq n ≔ λn. λh. ?g2

-- a sum behind a definition, likewise
def v : alias → ℕ using (alias.unfold) ≔ λx. ?g3
